.TH CJSON_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_set \- write a C scalar to the DOM leaf at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_set(cjson root, const char *path, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The macro finds the type and the raw value of val at compile time
with _Generic.  It then writes the value as a scalar to the leaf
node that path addresses.

Accepted C types for val:
  bool                       -> CJSON_BOOL
  any integer type           -> CJSON_INTEGER (stored as long long);
                                an unsigned value > LLONG_MAX
                                -> CJSON_FLOAT (stored as double)
  float / double             -> CJSON_FLOAT  (stored as double)
  char * / const char *      -> CJSON_STRING  (strdup'd copy)
  char array (char buf[N])   -> CJSON_STRING
  string literal ("...")     -> CJSON_STRING
  NULL, C23 nullptr          -> CJSON_NULL

Before C23 the macro true is the int 1, so cjson_set(doc, k, true)
stores the CJSON_INTEGER 1.  Write (bool)true for a CJSON_BOOL.  In C23
true has the type bool and needs no cast. The library headers do not
include <stdbool.h> under C23; a C library whose <stdbool.h> still
defines true as 1 there (FreeBSD 14) brings the int back into every
file that includes it.

The macro copies val into a local whose type is the type of val after
array-to-pointer conversion, with top-level qualifiers removed.  A
char array, such as a buffer that snprintf(3) filled, therefore
arrives as a pointer to its first character, and a pointer that is
itself const, such as a static const char *const, arrives as an
ordinary pointer.  Each one gives the text of the string.

cjson_set rejects all other C types.  This includes long double, a
struct, and all pointer types other than char * and const char *.  For
such a type, cjson_set gives ccol_invalid_args and does not change the
target leaf.  It does not create a new leaf.  It does not change the
value of a leaf that exists.

cjson_set also rejects a void * value that is not NULL, with
ccol_invalid_args.  Only a NULL value writes CJSON_NULL.  This is true
although void * is also the compile-time type of the bare NULL
literal.

A char *, const char * or string-literal value must be valid UTF-8.
JSON text is UTF-8 (RFC 8259 section 8.1), so any other byte
sequence has no JSON form.  cjson_set returns ccol_invalid_args and
does not touch the target leaf.  This is the same behaviour as for a
double that is not finite.  The same rule holds for the leaf
component of the path itself.  That component becomes an object key
when the library has to create the leaf.  Every key that the tree
holds is valid UTF-8, so the library refuses a key that is not,
instead of storing it.  cjson_get(3) and cjson_delete(3) need no
matching rule.  They only look a component up.  A component that is
not valid UTF-8 can never match a key that is already there, so they
report it as absent.

Signed integer types are sign-extended to long long correctly.
Unsigned integers in the range [0, LLONG_MAX] round-trip faithfully.
An unsigned value > LLONG_MAX, which only a 64-bit unsigned type
such as uint64_t can hold, has no long long form.  The macro stores
it as the CJSON_FLOAT nearest to it, which is what cjson_parse(3)
makes of the same decimal literal.  Read such a leaf with
cjson_double_val(3).  A double holds an integer exactly only up to
2^53, so the low digits of such a value can be lost.

The macro creates the leaf if it does not exist.  The immediate
parent of the leaf must exist.  If the leaf exists, the macro updates
that node in place.  Therefore, a handle to the node stays valid.  The
macro gives the node the type of val, whatever its current type is.
If the node is an array or an object, the macro deep-frees its
children before it writes the new value.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
root   Root cjson node.
path   Dot-separated path string to the target leaf.
       Escape sequences: '\e.' -> literal '.', '\e\e' -> literal '\e'.
       A '\e' before any other character is left unchanged.
val    C value; the type must be one of the accepted types listed above.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t: ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args; an empty path or path component (leading,
                     trailing, or consecutive dots), parent type
                     incompatible with the leaf component, a
                     malformed array index (non-numeric, negative,
                     or bare '#' with no digits), a path component
                     that is not valid UTF-8, a string value that is
                     not valid UTF-8, val's C type is not one of the
                     accepted types listed above, or val is a void *
                     value that is not NULL.
ccol_key_not_found; the parent path, or a syntactically valid but
                     out-of-range array index, does not exist.  An
                     array, unlike an object, has no way to
                     auto-extend to fit an arbitrary index.
ccol_not_enough_memory; allocation failure.
ccol_container_full; the parent object has already reached its
                     maximum element count (creating a new key only).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A bool literal needs an explicit cast: (bool)true.  Without the cast
the macro sees the integer value 1 and not _Bool.  The result is
then CJSON_INTEGER and not CJSON_BOOL.

Plain char takes the default signedness of the platform.  It is
signed on x86/x86-64 Linux.  It is unsigned where -funsigned-char
applies, for example on ARM.  Write signed char or unsigned char
explicitly when portability is important.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.active", (bool)true);
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.score",  99);
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.label",  "champion");
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.ratio",  0.75);

/* Type change: replace an existing object subtree with a scalar */
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.metadata", 0);  /* deep-frees old subtree */

/* Set to null */
cjson_set(doc, "users.#0.deprecated", NULL);
.fi
